    "title": "pandas.Index.droplevel — pandas 2.3.3 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Index.droplevel#\n\n- \n*final* Index.droplevel(*level=0* )[source]#\n- Return index with requested level(s) removed. If resulting index has only 1 level left, the result will be of Index type, not MultiIndex. The original index is not modified inplace. \n  - Parameters:\n    - **level** int, str, or list-like, default 0\n    - If a string is given, must be the name of a level If list-like, elements must be names or indexes of levels.\n  - Returns:\n    - Index or MultiIndex\n Examples >>> mi = pd.MultiIndex.from_arrays( ... [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]], names=['x', 'y', 'z']) >>> mi MultiIndex([(1, 3, 5), (2, 4, 6)], names=['x', 'y', 'z']) >>> mi.droplevel() MultiIndex([(3, 5), (4, 6)], names=['y', 'z']) >>> mi.droplevel(2) MultiIndex([(1, 3), (2, 4)], names=['x', 'y']) >>> mi.droplevel('z') MultiIndex([(1, 3), (2, 4)], names=['x', 'y']) >>> mi.droplevel(['x', 'y']) Index([5, 6], dtype='int64', name='z')",
    "markdown_length": 913,
